Q: Having one set of variables for both types of iterators in C++ A: <blockquote>
<p>How do I declare them and initialize depending on n?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The problem is that <code>begin()</code> and <code>end()</code> return a <code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;::iterator</code>, whereas <code>rbegin()</code> and <code>rend()</code> return a <code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;::reverse_iterator</code>. These are different types, and your <code>it_b</code>,<code>it_e</code> cannot be both.</p>
<p>One solution is to use an intermediate function template, where the type of iterator is the template parameter.</p>
<p>This is demonstrated below:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

template &lt;typename IterT&gt;
void my_loop(IterT const&amp; from, IterT const&amp; to) {
    for (IterT it = from; it != to; it++) {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; *it &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot;;
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
}

void test(std::vector&lt;int&gt; const &amp; nums, int n) {
    if (n &gt;= 0) {
        my_loop(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    }
    else {
        my_loop(nums.rbegin(), nums.rend());
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector&lt;int&gt; nums = { 1,2,3,4 };
    test(nums, 1);
    test(nums, -1);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>1 2 3 4
4 3 2 1
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/1ns67f19E" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo</a></p>
<p>If you want to make <code>my_loop</code> more generic, you can add another template parameter for which you can pass a lambda or any other callable.</p>

Q: Show data from SQL database in Gridview A: <p>Because of your model it doesn't show anything. Webgrid doesn't know how to render your Artist or Album or Genre table model. You need to create new model with basic variables like string, int, decimal</p>
<pre><code>public ActionResult Show1()
{
    var query = from a in DB.Album
                join b in DB.Artists
                on a.ArtistId equals b.ArtistId
                join c in DB.Genre
                on a.GenreId equals c.GenreId
                where (b.ArtistId == 2)
                select new common { AlbumName = a.AlbumName, ArtistName = b.ArtistName, GenreName = c.GenreName /* ... other props */ };
    return View(query.ToList());
}

public class common
{

    public string ArtistName { get; set; }
    public string AlbumName { get; set; }
    public string GenreName { get; set; }
    //you need to use basic variables like string, int, decimal...

}
</code></pre>

Q: High severity transitive vulnerability in a popular Microsoft package? A: <p>I am not sure what you mean by &quot;tried to install&quot;.</p>
<p>Usually, you would add something like this to your <code>.csproj</code></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;!-- Forced upgrade due to vulnerability --&gt;
    &lt;PackageReference Include=&quot;Microsoft.OpenApi&quot; Version=&quot;3.8.0&quot; /&gt; &lt;!-- Dep. of Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi --&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then try to build again. It <em>may</em> be necessary to clean and build or explicitly <code>dotnet restore</code>.</p>
<hr />
<p>If you are using CPM ( <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/nuget/consume-packages/central-package-management" rel="noreferrer">Central Package Management</a> ) the story is slightly different but basically the same: You explicitly force an upgrade of the dependency.</p>

Q: How to convert an HTMLElement to a string A: <p>The element <code>outerHTML</code> property (note: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/element.outerHTML" rel="noreferrer">supported by Firefox after version 11</a>) returns the HTML of the entire element.</p>
<h1>Example</h1>
<pre><code>&lt;div id=&quot;new-element-1&quot;&gt;Hello world.&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>&lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot;&gt;

    var element = document.getElementById(&quot;new-element-1&quot;);
    var elementHtml = element.outerHTML;
    // &lt;div id=&quot;new-element-1&quot;&gt;Hello world.&lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Similarly, you can use <code>innerHTML</code> to get the HTML contained within a given element, or <code>innerText</code> to get the text inside an element (sans HTML markup).</p>
<h1>See Also</h1>
<ol>
<li><a href="http://javascript.gakaa.com/element-outerhtml.aspx" rel="noreferrer">outerHTML - Javascript Property</a></li>
<li><a href="http://javascript.gakaa.com/c/element.aspx" rel="noreferrer">Javascript Reference - Elements</a></li>
</ol>

Q: react-native-maps: Custom marker images display inconsistently, animations fail on custom markers, and looking for alternative map packages A: <p>When standard marker components are rendered, the underlying native SDKs capture a single cache snapshot of the React View. If a remote image or SVG finishes loading after this snapshot is captured, the marker remains blank.</p>
<p>Set <code>tracksViewChanges={true}</code> initially. Once the custom image's <code>onLoad</code> event triggers, toggle <code>tracksViewChanges</code> to <code>false</code> to freeze the rendering snapshot and let the processor idle:</p>
<pre><code>const CustomMarker = ({ coordinate, imageSource }) =&gt; {
  // 1. Set tracksViewChanges={true} initially
  const [shouldTrackChanges, setShouldTrackChanges] = useState(true);

  return (
    &lt;Marker
      coordinate={coordinate}
      tracksViewChanges={shouldTrackChanges}
    &gt;
      &lt;Image
        source={imageSource}
        style={{ width: 40, height: 40 }}
        // 2. Once the custom image's onLoad event triggers
        onLoad={() =&gt; {
          // 3. toggle tracksViewChanges to false to freeze the rendering snapshot
          setShouldTrackChanges(false);
        }}
      /&gt;
    &lt;/Marker&gt;
  );
};
</code></pre>
<p>then animate the coordinates directly using the map's native <code>AnimatedRegion</code> API or <code>Marker.Animated</code> instead of standard layout transforms.</p>
<p>Animating standard layout properties (like <code>scale</code> or <code>opacity</code> inside <code>&lt;Marker&gt;</code>) causes the React hierarchy to constantly redraw, resulting in severe lag, malformed rendering, or complete disappearing acts.</p>
